SUMMARY:SPACE FARMS!
DESCRIPTION:SPACE FARMS!\n\nWhat will our lunar engineers eat while 
 establishing permanence on the Moon? How will we grow mushrooms on Mars? 
 Can cotton germinate in space? How does space agriculture benefit us on 
 Earth? \n\nJoin Andrew Rose for an exciting\, family-friendly exploration 
 into the rapidly evolving world of space agriculture where science fiction 
 is science fact!\n\nInspired by work from organizations like NASA and 
 innovative and inspiring companies around the globe (like Interstellar 
 Lab)\, this interactive talk will introduce audiences to the fascinating 
 challenge of growing food beyond our planet. From leafy greens grown aboard 
 the International Space Station to futuristic systems that can produce 
 protein from air\, the future of farming is being written. \n\nDiscover: 
 \n\n● How scientists are growing plants in zero/micro gravity \n\n● Why 
 crops like lettuce\, radishes\, and mushrooms are leading the way \n\n● 
 How space farming technologies are already improving agriculture here on 
 Earth \n\n● What it will take to sustain human permanence on the Moon and 
 Mars \n\nThis event is designed to spark curiosity and imagination in 
 students of all ages. Young scholars are invited to think like scientists\, 
 engineers\, and astronauts as we explore real-world problems (and 
 opportunities) in feeding humanity beyond Earth. \n\nWhether you're a 
 student\, parent\, educator\, or simply curious about the future\, this 
 talk will leave you with a new perspective on how food\, technology\, and 
 exploration are deeply connected. \n\nThe same technologies (controlled 
 environment agriculture) that will feed astronauts in space will help solve 
 some of the biggest challenges on Earth like climate change\, food 
 security\, and resource scarcity. \n\n\n\nAndrew Rose is an agricultural 
 futurist. He focuses on projects that benefit people whose parents haven't 
 been born yet. He is an advisor at the BIO-ISAC (www.isac.bio)\, helping to 
 bring cybersecurity (and related threat) awareness/mitigation/response to 
 the bioeconomy with a focus on the agriculture and food sector. He has 
 served as the Chief Strategy Officer in Residence at the Emerging 
 Technologies Centers\, the Chair of the Advisory Council for the Maryland 
 Cybersecurity Association\, and a judge for the first two years of the 
 Grow-NY AgInnovation in conjunction with Cornell. Andrew helped found the 
 GIVE Program\, a leadership training program in Maryland for rising 
 professionals. \n\n\n\nBefore the event fly by the Innovation Lab for Space 
 Agriculture - A Simulation in Stations a drop in event exploring what it is 
 like to research plant life and try to garden in outer space on a space 
 station! \n\nJoin the Maker   STEAM team to explore 4 stations of short 
 activities simulating parts of what space research about agriculture\, 
 plants\, and gardening looks like on a space station. In these stations\, 
 you can use NASA information to: \n\n\n	\n	Pick a (pretend) plant to grow 
 \n	\n\n\n\n	\n	Set up the growing environment \n	\n\n\n\n	\n	"Water" the 
 plant \n	\n\n\n\n	\n	Measure the plant's growth \n	\n\n\nEach of these 
 stations will be simulations\, no actual planting occurring this time! 
 These activities are geared towards ages 5 and up\, all ages welcome. 
 \n\nThis program is drop-in\, no registration required. Please join us 
 between 5pm and 6pm in the Innovation Lab on 3rd floor of Main Library. The 
 stations will take most participants around 20 to 30 minutes to complete. 
 This event is for all ages\; children under age 11 must be accompanied by 
 an adult. \n\n \n\nWednesday June 24th starting at 6 pm in the Main Library 
 Auditorium located at 300 North Roxboro Street in Durham.  \n\n\n\nParking 
 is available in the library parking lot or at the North Queen Street Garage 
 located at 115 North Queen Street which is behind the apartments directly 
 across the street from the library. Parking at the garage is free. Please 
 bring your parking ticket to a service point for validation.        
 \n\nRegistration is appreciated to receive an email reminder\, cancellation 
 link\, and program updates. Seating is first come first served. 
 Registration does not hold or guarantee a seat.      \n\n  \n\nQuestions? 
 Email mkoslofsky@dconc.gov \n\n \n\nImage description: A comic book panel 
 illustration from the 1950’s depicting a male astronaut looking out over 
 an expanse of crops being tended by futuristic space technology.
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Megan Koslofsky":MAILTO:mkoslofsky@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Classes & Lectures, Durham, Environmental, Health & Fitness, Innovation Lab, Maker, Social, STEAM
